  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Iron Age islanders built a wall around a meteorite crater. Kaali, on Saaremaa, off the coast of Estonia.",
        "visual_query": "estonian island rural stone wall",
        "visual_fallback": "stone wall field"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The main crater is 110 metres wide and 22 metres deep, now filled with water. Eight smaller ones lie within a kilometre.",
        "visual_query": "round crater lake aerial estonia",
        "visual_fallback": "crater lake"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The rock came in at up to 20 kilometres a second and broke apart in the air. The blast was close to a small nuclear bomb.",
        "visual_query": "meteor fireball night sky",
        "visual_fallback": "night meteor"
      },
      {
        "narration": "And people were living there when it hit, though nobody agrees when that was, with estimates running from about 7,600 to 3,000 years ago. So what do you build after the sky explodes?",
        "visual_query": "bronze age northern european settlement",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient settlement"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A 470-metre stone wall around the lake, with animal bones heaped inside, horses, sheep, oxen. Estonians called it the holy lake.",
        "visual_query": "baltic island forest crater lake",
        "visual_fallback": "forest lake"
      }
    ],
